\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Eliminating alternate vertices of the four-cycle}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{In the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), take #{t,m>0} and
#{Q=Q_{t,m}=L_t+m^2I}. Retain #{R=(0,2)} and eliminate #{U=(1,3)},
with coordinates #{x=(\phi_0,\phi_2)} and #{y=(\phi_1,\phi_3)} in
these orders, using the [product splitting](fdip-002J). Write #{a=m^2} and #{s=1+t+a}. Reordering the
[explicit Laplacian](fdip-0008) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
A=C&=sI_2,\\
H&=-\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\t&1\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
The [elimination theorem](fdip-000D) therefore gives
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&-\beta\\-\beta&\alpha\end{pmatrix},\\
\alpha&=s-\frac{1+t^2}{s},\qquad
\beta=\frac{2t}{s}>0.
\end{aligned}}
Indeed #{HH^T} has diagonal #{1+t^2} and off-diagonal #{2t}, so
#{S=sI_2-s^{-1}HH^T}.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5}{boyd2004convex}, pp. 650-651, gives the
general \vocabk{Schur complement}{fdip-000D}. Here the chosen blocks make its entries
explicit.}

\tikzfigs{0.55}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  kept/.style={circle,draw,thick,minimum size=7mm,inner sep=0pt},
  removed/.style={circle,draw,dashed,minimum size=7mm,inner sep=0pt},
  edge/.style={thick}
]
\node[kept] (v0) at (0,3) {$0$};
\node[removed] (v1) at (3.2,3) {$1$};
\node[kept] (v2) at (3.2,0.8) {$2$};
\node[removed] (v3) at (0,0.8) {$3$};
\draw[edge] (v0) -- node[above=2mm] {$1$} (v1);
\draw[edge] (v1) -- node[right=2mm] {$t$} (v2);
\draw[edge] (v2) -- node[below=2mm] {$1$} (v3);
\draw[edge] (v3) -- node[left=2mm] {$t$} (v0);
\draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (1.6,0.1) -- (1.6,-0.6);
\node[right=2mm] at (1.6,-0.25) {integrate $1,3$};
\node[kept] (r0) at (0,-1.5) {$0$};
\node[kept] (r2) at (3.2,-1.5) {$2$};
\draw[edge] (r0) -- node[above=2mm] {$\beta=2t/s$} (r2);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Solid circles are retained; dashed circles are integrated out.
Orientations are suppressed because the quadratic energy is
[orientation independent](fdip-0008). The bottom edge represents
the induced coupling #{\beta}; its precision also has the diagonal
term computed below.}

\p{The vectors #{(1,1)^T/\sqrt2} and #{(1,-1)^T/\sqrt2} diagonalize
#{S}. Their eigenvalues are
##{\begin{aligned}
\sigma_+&=\alpha-\beta
 =\frac{s^2-(1+t)^2}{s}\\
 &=\frac{a(a+2+2t)}{s},\\
\sigma_-&=\alpha+\beta
 =\frac{s^2-(1-t)^2}{s}\\
 &=\frac{(a+2)(a+2t)}{s}.
\end{aligned}}
Both are positive. The factorizations follow from #{s=1+t+a};
they also check positivity without appealing again to the general
theorem. Put
##{\mathcal D=a(a+2)(a+2t)(a+2+2t).}
Then
##{\begin{aligned}
\det C&=s^2,\qquad \det S=\mathcal D/s^2,\\
\det Q&=\det C\det S=\mathcal D,\\
c_C&=\frac{2\pi}{s},\qquad
Z_S=\frac{2\pi s}{\sqrt{\mathcal D}},\\
Z_Q&=c_CZ_S=\frac{(2\pi)^2}{\sqrt{\mathcal D}}.
\end{aligned}}
This reproduces the [four-mode determinant and partition function](fdip-0037).
The \vocabk{effective action}{fdip-000D} is
##{\mathcal A_{\mathrm{eff}}(x)=\tfrac12x^TSx+\log\frac{s}{2\pi}.}
Its constant records the integrated block.}

\p{A retained observable checks more than normalization. For
#{F(x)=x_0x_2}, polynomial integrability and the
[marginal identity](fdip-000E) give
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_Q
 &=\langle x_0x_2\rangle_S
  =(S^{-1})_{02}\\
 &=\frac{\beta}{\alpha^2-\beta^2}
  =\frac{2ts}{\mathcal D}.
\end{aligned}}
The indices #{0,2} here name retained vertices. Inverting the displayed
two-by-two matrix gives its off-diagonal entry
#{\frac{\beta}{\det S}}, which proves the last equalities. Independently, the
[four-cycle spectral table](fdip-0037) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
4\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_Q
 &=\frac1a-\frac1{a+2}\\
 &\quad-\frac1{a+2t}+\frac1{a+2+2t}\\
 &=\frac{8ts}{\mathcal D}.
\end{aligned}}
For the last equality, pair the first two terms and the last two:
their difference has numerator
#{2((a+2t)(a+2+2t)-a(a+2))=8ts} over #{\mathcal D}.
At #{t=m=1}, one obtains
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}7/3&-2/3\\-2/3&7/3\end{pmatrix},\\
\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_Q&=\frac{2}{15}.
\end{aligned}}
Fixing the eliminated fields to zero would instead give precision
#{A=3I_2} and zero off-diagonal covariance.}

\p{There was no edge between vertices #{0} and #{2} in the supplied
cycle. Elimination induces one in the effective quadratic form:
##{S=\beta\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}
       +\sigma_+I_2.}
Thus #{\beta} is an effective edge weight and #{\sigma_+} an effective
mass coefficient. This is a finite instance of fill-in: eliminating
a single vertex joined to retained vertices with weights #{w_i}
and diagonal precision #{c>0} subtracts #{ww^T/c}, creating
off-diagonal entries #{-w_iw_j/c} where no retained edge existed.
The formula is the same Schur complement with #{H=-w} and #{C=(c)}.
Locality in the original graph need not survive elimination.}

\p{The half-turn preserves both sets #{R,U} and swaps the two
coordinates within each. For the swap matrix #{J}, the displayed
#{S} satisfies #{JSJ=S}; the induced two-site form retains this
symmetry. At #{m=0}, however, #{C=(1+t)I_2} is still positive while
#{\sigma_+=0}. The fiber factor is finite, but the integral over
retained fields diverges along #{(1,1)}. A finite fiber integral
does not imply a finite partition function. The
[global mean-zero law](fdip-000C) uses a constraint coupling #{x}
and #{y}, so its measure is not the product #{dx\,dy} used here.
It cannot be obtained by substituting #{m=0} in this marginal formula.}
